--- Hostel Wireshark Graph Analysis ---
  - Initial ssthresh = 16 (doubled 1->2->4->8->16, then +1 linear from RTT 5 to 8).
  - After RTT 8 (cwnd=19): ssthresh = floor(19/2) = 9 (3 dup ACKs -> Fast Recovery, cwnd=9).
  - After RTT 14 (cwnd=14): ssthresh = floor(14/2) = 7 (Timeout RTO -> cwnd drops to 1).
  - Sender variant: TCP Reno (exhibits Fast Recovery at RTT 8, unlike Tahoe which drops to 1 on every loss).
  - Throughput at RTT 7 (cwnd=18, MSS=1460 B, RTT=40 ms): 5.256 Mbps
  - AIMD convergence: Additive Increase (+1/RTT) + Multiplicative Decrease (/2) shrinks the larger
    flow by more segments on loss, driving both flows toward the 45-degree equal-share line.

--- Alternative Concept: 1% Random (Non-Congestion) Loss — Reno vs BBR ---
  - Reno treats every 1% random loss as congestion and halves cwnd ~every 100 pkts (Mathis limit ~0.87*MSS/(RTT*sqrt(p))).
  - BBR paces at estimated bottleneck BW x min_RTT without halving rate on isolated random loss -> far higher throughput.
  - Hostel Server Recommendation: Use BBR for high-BDP Wi-Fi/WAN downloads with random loss (CUBIC if shallow FIFO fairness needed).
